  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The <b>correct answer</b> is: Leverage the AWS CLI deploy register command to create an IAM user dedicated to the on-premises server, store the IAM user’s credentials in a config file on the server, and register it with CodeDeploy.<br><br>AWS CodeDeploy requires that on-premises instances are registered as deployment targets. The recommended and supported process includes creating an IAM user, assigning the necessary permissions, saving the access keys in the instance’s configuration file, and using the <b>register</b> command to officially connect the server to CodeDeploy.<br><br><i>\"Create an IAM role for the on-premises server and assign CodeDeploy permissions. Register the server with CodeDeploy by binding the IAM role using the AWS CLI register command.\"</i>: IAM roles cannot be assumed by on-premises servers; roles are designed for AWS services and instances, not external servers. The supported authentication method for on-premises resources is via IAM users.<br><br><i>\"Add the IAM user to the CodeDeploy configuration file, install the CodeDeploy agent on the server, and allow the agent to automatically complete server registration.\"</i>: While installing the CodeDeploy agent is critical, simply placing an IAM user in the config file does not ensure registration. You must still use the register command to finish the process.<br><br><i>\"Use the AWS CLI deploy tag-resource command to create an IAM user for the on-premises server, register the server with CodeDeploy, and assign a tag to it. CodeDeploy will then deploy new versions to servers based on specified tags.\"</i>: The <b>tag-resource</b> command does not perform instance registration. Registration and tagging are separate steps.<br><br><b>Tip:</b> For on-premises deployments with CodeDeploy, always authenticate with IAM user credentials and use the proper registration workflow for secure, managed deployment.<br></p>",
  "query": "<p>Your company operates an Ubuntu server located on-premises in the local data center, where multiple legacy apps are hosted.<br>You wish to utilize AWS CodeDeploy to roll out updated app revisions to this specific server.<br>However, before doing so, you must register the instance with CodeDeploy and authenticate the process using an IAM user.<br>Which approach should you choose?</p>",
